{"case_id":"C025","gap_exists":true,"gap_type":["entry_point","state","input_semantics","attack_surface"],"commit_induced":"YES","maintenance_needed":true,"confidence":99,"affected_functions":["ecma_hasher.update!","ecma_hasher.update_u64!","ecma_hasher.up!","ecma_hasher.up_x86_sse42!"],"reason":"H0 exclusively drives the JSON decoder through fuzz_cpp, fuzz_complex, and fuzz_simple; it never constructs or invokes crc64.ecma_hasher. S1 introduces a new architecture-specific CRC64 execution path, selected in ecma_hasher.update! when this.state == 0 and implemented by ecma_hasher.up_x86_sse42!, with distinct alignment, length, SIMD-loop, reduction, and tail behavior. Consequently S1+H0 cannot reach or control this new path. Although CRC64 was already outside H0 in S0, the new x86_sse42 implementation materially expands the uncovered attack surface, so the material gap is commit-induced.","evidence":["H0 defines only WUFFS_CONFIG__MODULE__AUX__JSON and WUFFS_CONFIG__MODULE__JSON and calls wuffs_json__decoder__decode_tokens or wuffs_aux::DecodeJson; no crc64 module, ecma_hasher, update!, or update_u64! appears.","S1 adds choose up = [up_x86_sse42] inside ecma_hasher.update!, guarded by this.state == 0.","S1 adds ecma_hasher.up_x86_sse42!, which handles pointer alignment, inputs shorter than 0x20, 16-byte SIMD chunks, Barrett reduction, and a bytewise tail.","The S0 implementation had only the table-based ecma_hasher.update! path, so up_x86_sse42! and its input-sensitive behavior did not exist for H0 to miss."],"recommended_action":"Add or extend a CRC64 harness that initializes ecma_hasher and fuzzes update!/update_u64! across zero, short, aligned, unaligned, multi-block, and tail lengths, including multiple updates to exercise nonzero state; compare the selected x86_sse42 result with the generic up! result."}
